WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.928 --> 00:00:06.874
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:17.920
JOE BLITZSTEIN: The town of Bayesville has a population of 2000 people.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.380
Jimmy is worried, after reading a news article,

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.819
about a disease called conditionitis.

00:00:24.819 --> 00:00:27.520
Conditionitis is a serious, but rare, disease

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.180
afflicting 1% of the population.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:36.110
Let's represented healthy people as blue and diseased people as orange.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:40.470
Jimmy decides to go to the doctor to get tested.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:44.110
Unfortunately, Jimmy tests positive for the disease.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:47.610
In other words, the test says that he has conditionitis.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:51.760
We will represent positive test results with yellow hats.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.070
JIMMY: Could the test be wrong?

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:57.630
DOCTOR: Well, the test is 95% accurate.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:00.450
JOE BLITZSTEIN: What does this mean for Jimmy?

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:03.760
Note that there are two ways this test could be wrong.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.560
A person could test positive for the disease when they don't have it,

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.430
or they could test negative when they do have the disease.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:14.760
There are also two ways that the tests could be right.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.910
A person who has the disease could test positive,

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:22.230
or a person who does not have the disease could test negative.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:27.510
Suppose that 95% accurate means if a person has conditionitis,

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.630
than 95% of the time they will test positive,

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:33.280
which would be the correct diagnosis.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.230
If the person does not have conditionitis, then 95% of the time

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:42.720
they will test negative, which again would be the correct diagnosis.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.590
In other words, there is a 5% chance of misdiagnosis in each case.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:54.260
Given this information, how sure are you that Jimmy has the disease?

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:57.850
A. He almost certainly has the disease.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.740
B. He probably has the disease.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:04.260
C. It's about 50/50 whether he has the disease.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:09.020
or D. He probably does not have the disease.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:13.580
Let's imagine that we conduct the test for every single person in Bayesville,

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.790
and assign each person who tests positive for conditionitis

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:19.970
a yellow hat.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:22.040
Recall that healthy people are represented

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:26.150
as blue, diseased people are represented as orange,

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:30.590
and a yellow hat represents a positive test result.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:34.040
Since the test is not 100% accurate, some

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:39.220
of the people who get hats probably don't actually have conditionitis.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.900
The combination of the test results and the true disease status

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.480
classifies our population into four groups--

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.990
true negatives, or healthy individuals who are correctly

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:55.930
tested as negative, true positives, or diseased individuals who are correctly

00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:01.490
tested as positive, false negatives, or diseased individuals who

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:06.520
are incorrectly tested as negative, and false positives,

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:11.610
or healthy individuals who are incorrectly tested as positive.

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:14.990
If we just look at the negative results, our test seems pretty good.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:18.260
After all, it only missed one diseased person.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:21.450
However, looking at the positive test results,

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.320
we find that a large majority of the positive test results

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.720
are actually false alarms.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.690
This is good news for Jimmy.

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:34.640
His positive test result does increase his chance of being diseased.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.190
But with so many false positives, it turns out

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:40.880
that there's only a 16% chance he's actually sick.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.350
There's an 84% chance he does not have conditionitis.

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:52.220
So Jimmy stops panicking, but decides to get a second opinion just in case.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:53.810
Did you make the right prediction?

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.710
In general, we need to take into account the rarity of a disease,

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:03.460
not just the accuracy of a test.

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:07.720
And we need to distinguish between the probability of having a disease, given

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:11.847
that you test positive, and the probability that you test positive,

00:04:11.847 --> 00:04:13.180
given that you have the disease.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:16.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

